   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_Bf9RMnVKGkWbZK4k",
   "name": "Acceleration",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Acceleration"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- An object can change its speed by:\n\n  - speeding up\n  - slowing down\n- When an object speeds up, it is **accelerating**\n- When an object slows down, it is **decelerating**"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_qvDXDvpQbNhxtfc6",
   "name": "Calculating acceleration",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Calculating acceleration"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Extended Tier Only"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- When an object moves in a straight line, its acceleration can be defined as:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**The change in speed per unit time**"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- In other words, acceleration is the rate of change of speed\n- The acceleration of an object often changes throughout an object's journey\n- Therefore, it is often useful to know the **average acceleration**"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$a=\\frac{\\Deltav}{\\Deltat}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where:\n\n  - $a$ = acceleration in metres per second squared (m/s<sup>2</sup>)\n  - $\\Deltav$ = change in speed in metres per second (m/s)\n  - $\\Deltat$ = time taken in seconds (s)"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Formula triangle for acceleration, change in speed and change in time"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/08/1-2-2-acceleration-triangle-cie-igcse-23-rn.png",
   "file": "assets/f15fa20142d2-1-2-2-acceleration-triangle-cie-igcse-23-rn.png",
   "alt": "1-2-2-acceleration-triangle-cie-igcse-23-rn",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***To use a formula triangle, simply cover up the quantity you wish calculate and the structure of the equation is revealed***"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Change in speed"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **change in speed **is the **difference** between the initial and final speed:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\Deltav=v-u$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where:\n\n  - $\\Deltav$ = change in speed in metres per second (m/s)\n  - $v$ = final speed in metres per second (m/s)\n  - $u$ = initial speed in metres per second (m/s)"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If an object speeds up, its acceleration is **positive**\n- If an object slows down, its acceleration is **negative**\n- Acceleration is positive if it is in the **same direction** as the motion of the object"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Acceleration of different objects"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2021/07/5.6.10-Acceleration-Examples.png",
   "file": "assets/3f79363e3830-5-6-10-acceleration-examples.png",
   "alt": "Acceleration Examples, downloadable IGCSE & GCSE Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***A rocket speeding up (accelerating) and a car slowing down (decelerating)***"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "A Japanese bullet train decelerates at a constant rate in a straight line. The speed of the train decreases from 50 m/s to 42 m/s in 30 seconds.\n\n(a) Calculate the change in speed of the train.\n\n(b) Calculate the deceleration of the train, and explain how your answer shows the train is slowing down.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**Part (a)**\n\n**Step 1: List the known quantities**\n\n- Initial speed, $u=50m/s$\n- Final speed, $v=42m/s$\n\n**Step 2: Write the equation for change in speed **\n\n$\\Deltav=v-u$\n\n**Step 3: Substitute values for final and initial speed **\n\n$\\Deltav=42-50$\n\n$\\Deltav=-8m/s$\n\n**Part (b)**\n\n**Step 1: List the known quantities**\n\n- Change in speed, $\\Deltav=-8m/s$\n- Time taken, $\\Deltat=30s$\n\n**Step 2: Write the equation for acceleration**\n\n$a=\\frac{\\Deltav}{\\Deltat}$\n\n**Step 3: Substitute the values for change in speed and time**\n\n$a=\\frac{-8}{30}$\n\n$a=-0.27m/s^{2}$\n\n**Step 4: Interpret the value for deceleration**\n\n- The answer is **negative**, which indicates the train is **slowing down**"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "Remember, the units for acceleration are **metres per second squared**, m/s<sup>2</sup>. In other words, acceleration measures how much the speed (m/s) changes every second, so the units are metres per second per second (m/s/s).\n\nYou may see the term \"velocity\" also used to describe speed. Speed means how fast something is going (e.g. 5 m/s), whereas velocity means how fast something is going and in which direction (e.g 5 m/s in a north direction). You don't need to know about velocity for IGCSE GCSE Combined Science."
  }
 ]
}